The Racket binding loads the installed shared pancake archives and mirrors the
à-la-carte archive set. The core module
[`bindings/racket/pancake.rkt`](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/racket/pancake.rkt)
(over `libpancake_ocapn`) presents a `peer`, native Racket values, and event
structs. You `require` one module per transport — `pancake-tcp.rkt`,
`pancake-inproc.rkt`, and the lazy `pancake-iroh.rkt` — to add only that
transport's archive. The core is the reference shape for the whole binding
family, because it reads the way a
[Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) programmer expects.

## The same round-trip

The [Start building]($link.page('start')) round-trip — connect, fetch, call,
handle the reply — reads like this in Racket, where `conn-send` pumps the
connection and hands back the reply.

> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **tcp**
> ```scheme
> (require "pancake.rkt")
>
> ;; tcp: the peer is a place, a cleartext host and port.
> (define partner (tcp-locator "127.0.0.1" 9099))
>
> (define c (connect partner))
> (define greeter (conn-fetch c "greeter")) ; a capability, by name
> (printf "~a\n" (conn-send c greeter 'greet "world"))
> (connection-close! c)
> ```
> **iroh**
> ```scheme
> (require "pancake.rkt"
>          "pancake-iroh.rkt")
>
> ;; iroh reachability: the peer is a key, not a place. IROH-REACH-DISCOVERY
> ;; resolves the bare node id over the open internet; no address in hand.
> (define partner "ocapn://3b6a…da29.iroh")
>
> (define c (connect partner IROH-REACH-DISCOVERY))
> (define greeter (conn-fetch c "greeter")) ; a capability, by name
> (printf "~a\n" (conn-send c greeter 'greet "world"))
> (connection-close! c)
> ```

The iroh tab swaps the built host-and-port locator for a bare node id and adds
the posture constant with its `require`; everything below `connect` is the tcp
tab, line for line. The node id itself still arrives out of band, exactly like a
host and port; discovery turns a name you were given into a route, it does
not browse for peers.

## A Goblins-faithful actor layer

Racket keeps the Goblins verbs intact. You define an actor constructor with
`(define (^name bcom arg ...) ...)`, return its `methods` behavior, and then
`spawn` it. You
call it near with `$`, send it eventually with `<-` or `<-np`, observe a promise
with `on`, and become a new behavior with `bcom`, all inside `with-vat`. On top of
that, `connect`, `conn-fetch`, and `conn-send` reach a partner, and
`(accept (listen host port))` hands back the same `connection` that `connect`
returns.

> **Near-call (`$`) and eventual-send (`<-`)** — the two ways to invoke an object,
> straight from the [E language](http://erights.org/). `$` calls a near object now
> and returns its result, because it lives in your own vat. `<-` queues the call
> for a later turn and hands back a promise (`<-np` when you do not need the
> promise back). It is the only way to reach across a session, since a partner's
> object is never in your vat.

## Async

The async surface uses Racket's green threads and ports. An `async-connection`
drives the peer over a Racket port pair — `tcp-connect` on the dial side,
`accept` on the listening side — or a transport a companion module injects; you
read events as a sequence with `(in-async-events conn)`, and `(on vow handler)`
fires when a promise settles.

## Dogfood and example

```sh
zig build racket-dogfood    # the Racket consumer against the pancake archives
```

The full surface lives in the
[Racket binding README](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/racket/README.md),
and the
[rooms chat client](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/apps/rooms/racket/room-client.rkt)
builds a small capability-secure room on this surface.
